{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"write-play-for-kids-to-perform","kind":"prompt","title":"Write a play for kids to perform","description":"Writes a short play for children to rehearse and perform at home, in class or at a club, with parts sized to each child, simple staging and props, and a rehearsal plan.","category":"kids-activities","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan"],"role":["parent","teacher"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["preferences"],"output":["script","plan","checklist"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"quick","interaction":"one-shot","model_tier":"mid","reasoning":"optional","level":"beginner","depth":"specific","tags":["kids-theatre","drama-games","family-show","class-assembly","script-writing","rehearsal-plan"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["create-puppet-show","tell-hero-story-series","play-pretend-with-young-child"]},"args":[{"name":"cast","description":"How many children, their ages and anything that matters (a shy child, a non-reader, a child who loves to sing).","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"theme","description":"The story idea or theme, for example \"a dragon who is scared of the dark\", \"the water cycle\", or \"surprise me\".","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"length_minutes","description":"How long the performance should last, in minutes.","type":"number","default":10},{"name":"occasion","description":"Where and for whom, such as grandparents at home, a class assembly or a holiday club.","type":"text","required":false}],"output_contract":{"format":"markdown","sections":["Cast list","The script","Staging and props","Rehearsal plan","Performance day"]},"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"<context>\nYou write plays for children to perform. Children enjoy performing when every child has a moment, lines are short and repetitive enough to remember, and there is a chorus, song or action part for those who do not want to speak much. Non-readers can learn lines by repetition or play a part with actions. A narrator, played by an older child or adult, holds the story together and can prompt. Simple costumes and props from home, a clear beginning, problem and happy ending, and a funny moment make it work for both performers and audience.\n\n<cast>\n{{cast}}\n</cast>\n\n<theme>\n{{theme}}\n</theme>\n\nPerformance length in minutes: {{length_minutes}}\n\n{{#occasion}}Occasion: {{occasion}}{{/occasion}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. If the cast size or ages are missing, ask.\n2. Cast list: a part for every child with line count matched to age and confidence, including non-speaking or group parts for shy children and a narrator.\n3. The script: a complete play of about {{length_minutes}} minutes on the theme, with stage directions in brackets, short lines, a repeated refrain or chorus line, a song or movement moment, and a clear ending with a bow.\n4. Staging and props: a simple stage area, entrances, costumes and props from household items.\n5. Rehearsal plan: three to five short rehearsals with games to learn lines, blocking, a dress rehearsal, and how to handle nerves and forgotten lines (the narrator helps).\n6. Performance day: a running order, a welcome line for the audience, and a thank-you at the end.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Every child gets a moment; no child is left with only one line unless they asked for that.\n- Age-appropriate content; no frightening scenes for young casts.\n- Original story and songs; do not copy existing plays, films or copyrighted songs.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Cast list\nTable: Part | Who | Lines | Notes.\n## The script\n## Staging and props\nChecklist.\n## Rehearsal plan\n## Performance day\n</output_format>","steps":[]}
